{
  "file": "crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs",
  "item": "impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[node_content]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This storage-only consumer must delegate load/store using node_path\n without resolving contents. Graph requires the callback, but invoking\n this guard would violate the no-content-read invariant of the test.\n Other graph fixtures exercise content resolution."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation is on an explicit function with a body (`crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Graph]/fn[node_content]`). Its commentary is in fenced citation form, which the built-in accepts.\n\nThe explanation is substantive and matches the current source:\n- `node_content` is a required method of the `Graph` trait (`crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#trait[Graph]/fn[node_content]`), so `StoredGraph` must define it.\n- `StoredGraph` is a storage-only fixture. Its `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` delegate to `FileEvidenceStorage` using only `node_path`. The tests `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` and `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` use `StoredGraph` for exactly this.\n- `node_content` panics on purpose, so the test fails if anything reads contents. That is the no-content-read invariant the commentary describes. Running the guard would defeat its purpose.\n- Other fixtures do implement real `node_content` bodies: `Memory` in `tests/agent.rs` and `tests/validation.rs`, and `Application` in `tests/contracts.rs`. This supports the claim that other graph fixtures exercise content resolution.\n\nI found no gap in the citation.",
